{"reference":"Titus 1","book":"Titus","chapter":1,"status":"pending","sections":[{"kind":"chapter_overview","title":"Overview","blocks":[{"id":"block_1","text":"The letter introduces itself as from Paul to Titus and presents Titus’s task in Crete as completing unfinished work by appointing elders in each town. The text itself does not explain exactly how the plural “elders” relates to the singular “overseer” described next, so later office structures should not simply be read back into these terms.","verse_refs":["Titus 1:1-4","Titus 1:10","Titus 1:12-13","Titus 1:5","Titus 1:5-9"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_titus_001_ae8fb9e98fe21fd7e46bb659","reader_path_titus_001_bde54b11e19dc52273f41e3e"]}]} ,{"kind":"interpretive_questions","title":"Reading the Polemic Carefully","blocks":[{"id":"block_2","text":"The opponents are described through a cluster of accusations: rebellious speech, deception, disruption of households, pursuit of gain, myths, human commands, and concerns about purity. The passage does not require readers to treat every feature as a description of one clearly identifiable group. Likewise, “those of the circumcision” occurs within this polemic; it does not identify Jewish people as a whole or support collective blame.","verse_refs":["Titus 1:10","Titus 1:10-16","Titus 1:14","Titus 1:5-9","Titus 1:6-9"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_titus_001_94c3487cb58022f3f258c6ab"]},{"id":"block_3","text":"The saying about Cretans in verses 12–13 is a harsh ethnic generalization, even though the letter affirms it within its argument. Its traditional association with Epimenides is not explicit in Titus, and its original source and transmission cannot be fully recovered. Naming the stereotype is essential to reading the passage without turning its polemic into contempt for Cretan people.","verse_refs":["Titus 1:12-13"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_titus_001_8e6206815f3103ee029fd3a6"]},{"id":"block_4","text":"“To the pure all things are pure” is immediately framed by references to mind, conscience, confession, and works. In this passage it belongs to the dispute with the opponents; it is not a general permission to set aside ethical discernment, consent, material harm, or accountability. Similarly, the stated purpose of sharp rebuke is soundness in faith, not humiliation, violence, or authoritarian control.","verse_refs":["Titus 1:11-15","Titus 1:13","Titus 1:15-16","Titus 1:4","Titus 1:5"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_titus_001_c026b00faac542938f08648e"]}]},{"kind":"things_easy_to_miss","title":"Leadership and Authorship","blocks":[{"id":"block_5","text":"The leader profile emphasizes household reputation alongside hospitality, self-restraint, justice, holiness, discipline, and faithfulness to teaching. Its household language uses ancient gendered and domestic conventions and therefore requires contextual interpretation; it does not grant leaders exceptional status or immunity from scrutiny. Although Titus names Paul as sender, qualified scholars continue to disagree about authorship and about proposals involving a secretary or a Pauline school.","verse_refs":["Titus 1:1","Titus 1:1-4","Titus 1:15-16","Titus 1:6-9"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_titus_001_441fb0dbb0e532b319065be1"]}]},{"kind":"surrounding_passages","title":"Manuscript Note","blocks":[{"id":"block_6","text":"The surviving Codex Vaticanus ends before the Pastoral Epistles and is therefore not a manuscript witness to Titus.","verse_refs":["Titus 2:1-15","Titus 3:1-15"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_titus_001_f90d1b14c4a5ed8178552071"]}]}],"generated_metadata":null}